\subsection{Case Study: Polypropylene (PP)}

We present a detailed case study of the polypropylene optimization to illustrate the methodology and results.

\paragraph{Baseline.} Standard polypropylene (C$_3$H$_6$)$_n$ has 85.7\% C and 14.3\% H by weight. Commercial PP exhibits tensile strength of 30--40 MPa, Shore D hardness of 60--70, and melting point of 160--165°C.

\paragraph{Optimization.} The Chemical Carousel evaluated 1,976 candidates over 200 generations, with target properties of 600 MPa tensile strength, 1000 Shore D hardness, 80\% ductility, and 200°C melting point. The best candidate, designated \textbf{UBP-PP-A}, achieved an optimization score of 0.8304 (3.6\% improvement over baseline) with overall coherence of 0.7114.

\paragraph{Composition.} UBP-PP-A is a chlorofluoro-modified polypropylene copolymer with the following composition:

\begin{itemize}
    \item C: 86.2\%, H: 12.0\%, Cl: 0.48\%, F: 0.36\%, O: 0.35\%, N: 0.33\%, Si: 0.27\%
\end{itemize}

The trace amounts of heteroatoms (Cl, F, O, N, Si) represent functional comonomers that enhance properties without disrupting the polypropylene backbone.

